<article class="article">
<header class="article-head">
<p class="kicker"><span>Condition</span><span aria-hidden="true">·</span><a href="/condition/#sys-injuries">Injuries &amp; poisoning</a></p>
<h1>Injury to the Abdomen (Abdominal Trauma)</h1>
<p class="lead">An <strong>abdominal injury</strong> is damage to the belly wall or the organs inside it, from a blow (blunt trauma) such as a car crash, fall, sports collision or assault, or from a wound that pierces the skin (penetrating trauma). Bruising and pain may be all you see, yet the spleen, liver, bowel or kidneys can be bleeding inside. Any significant blow to the belly with worsening pain, dizziness or vomiting needs emergency assessment.</p>
<p class="also">Also called: Abdominal trauma, blunt abdominal trauma, belly injury, stomach injury, penetrating abdominal injury</p>
<p class="byline">By the <a href="/about/">iSymptom editorial team</a> · Updated <time datetime="2026-10-09">October 9, 2026</time> · 6 min read</p>
</header>
<div class="article-grid">
<aside class="article-side"><nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page"><details open><summary>On this page</summary><ol><li><a href="#what-to-do-right-away">What to do right away</a></li><li><a href="#abdominal-injury-meaning">Abdominal injury meaning</a></li><li><a href="#blunt-abdominal-injury-symptoms">Blunt abdominal injury symptoms</a></li><li><a href="#organs-commonly-injured">Organs commonly injured</a></li><li><a href="#how-doctors-diagnose-abdominal-injuries">How doctors diagnose abdominal injuries</a></li><li><a href="#treatment">Treatment</a></li><li><a href="#complications">Complications</a></li><li><a href="#recovery-and-returning-to-activity">Recovery and returning to activity</a></li><li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li></ol></details></nav></aside>
<div class="article-main">
<section class="glance" aria-label="At a glance"><h2 class="glance-title">At a glance</h2><dl><div><dt>What it means</dt><dd>Damage to the abdominal wall or organs from a blow or a penetrating wound.</dd></div><div><dt>Common causes</dt><dd>Car crashes (especially seat belt injuries), falls, bike handlebars, contact sports, assaults, stab and gunshot wounds.</dd></div><div><dt>Most often injured</dt><dd>Spleen and liver in blunt trauma. Small bowel and liver in stab wounds.</dd></div><div><dt>Danger signs</dt><dd>Worsening pain, a rigid belly, dizziness, fainting, vomiting blood or blood in the urine.</dd></div><div><dt>Diagnosis</dt><dd>Bedside ultrasound (FAST scan) and CT scan.</dd></div><div><dt>Treatment</dt><dd>Observation, embolization or surgery, depending on the organ and bleeding.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="triage" aria-labelledby="triage-h"><h2 id="triage-h" class="triage-title">When to get help</h2><div class="triage-grid"><div class="tri tri--red"><h3>Call 911 or go to the ER</h3><ul><li>Any stab, gunshot or impaling wound to the belly, back or lower chest. Do not remove an object stuck in the wound</li><li>Belly pain after an injury with dizziness, fainting, pale clammy skin or a fast heartbeat</li><li>A belly that becomes hard, swollen or very tender to touch</li><li>Vomiting blood, blood in the stool or urine after a blow</li><li>High-energy injury: car crash over 40 mph (65 km/h), ejection, a fall from more than twice your height, or being run over</li><li>Pain in the left shoulder tip after a blow to the left upper belly or ribs</li></ul></div><div class="tri tri--amber"><h3>See a doctor within a day or two</h3><ul><li>A seat belt bruise across the belly</li><li>A child who hit bike handlebars and has belly pain or a handlebar-shaped bruise</li><li>Belly pain that is slowly getting worse over hours after a fall or sports injury</li><li>Abdominal injury in someone who is pregnant or takes blood thinners</li></ul></div><div class="tri tri--blue"><h3>Book a routine appointment</h3><ul><li>Mild tenderness of the belly wall muscles after a minor knock, which is improving and with no other symptoms</li></ul></div><div class="tri tri--green"><h3>Usually safe to manage at home</h3><ul><li>A small bruise from a minor bump with no pain inside the belly, no dizziness and no vomiting, improving each day</li></ul></div></div></section>
<div class="prose">


<h2 id="what-to-do-right-away">What to do right away</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Call 911 or your local emergency number</strong> for any penetrating wound, high-speed crash, or worsening pain.</li><li><strong>Keep the person lying still</strong> and warm. Do not give food or drink, in case surgery is needed.</li><li><strong>Press firmly on bleeding wounds</strong> with a clean cloth.</li><li><strong>Leave impaled objects in place.</strong> Pad around them to stop movement.</li><li><strong>Cover protruding bowel</strong> with a clean, moist cloth or plastic wrap. Do not push it back in.</li><li><strong>Watch for shock:</strong> pale, cold, sweaty skin, confusion and a fast, weak pulse. Raise the legs if there is no spinal injury suspected.</li></ol>
<h2 id="abdominal-injury-meaning">Abdominal injury meaning</h2>
<p>In medical terms, abdominal trauma means injury to the area between the chest and pelvis, including the flanks and lower back, where the kidneys sit. Doctors split it into two kinds:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Blunt trauma</th><th scope="col">Penetrating trauma</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Cause</th><td>Crushing, deceleration or a direct blow</td><td>A sharp object or bullet breaks the skin</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Examples</th><td>Car crash, fall, kick, sports collision</td><td>Stab, gunshot, impalement</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Common organs hurt</th><td>Spleen, liver, kidneys, small bowel, pancreas</td><td>Liver, small bowel, colon, blood vessels</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Main challenge</th><td>Injuries are hidden and signs develop over hours</td><td>Obvious wound, but path inside is unclear</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Our <a href="/condition/abdominal-trauma/">abdominal trauma</a> page covers the trauma-team view in more detail.</p>
<h2 id="blunt-abdominal-injury-symptoms">Blunt abdominal injury symptoms</h2>
<p>Symptoms can be mild at first and build over hours as blood or bowel contents collect.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Abdominal pain</strong> that worsens rather than settles, or <a href="/symptom/sharp-abdominal-pain/">sharp abdominal pain</a> on moving or coughing</li><li><strong>Tenderness, guarding or rigidity:</strong> the belly muscles tighten over an injured organ</li><li><strong>Bruising,</strong> especially a seat belt mark across the lower belly or around the navel, or bruising in the flanks</li><li><strong>Swelling</strong> of the belly</li><li><strong>Nausea and vomiting,</strong> sometimes with blood</li><li><strong>Left shoulder tip pain</strong> (Kehr's sign) from blood irritating the diaphragm, a classic sign of spleen injury</li><li><strong><a href="/symptom/blood-in-urine/">Blood in the urine</a></strong> from kidney or bladder injury</li><li><strong>Signs of blood loss:</strong> dizziness, thirst, pale skin, rapid heartbeat, fainting</li></ul>
<p>People who are drunk, have a head injury, or have other painful injuries may not feel or report belly pain. That is why trauma teams scan anyone with a dangerous mechanism of injury.</p>
<h2 id="organs-commonly-injured">Organs commonly injured</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Spleen:</strong> the organ most often injured in blunt trauma. It bleeds heavily. See <a href="/condition/spleen-injury/">spleen injury</a>.</li><li><strong>Liver:</strong> large and fixed in place, so it tears in deceleration injuries. See <a href="/condition/liver-injury/">liver injury</a>.</li><li><strong>Kidneys:</strong> injured by blows to the back or flank, often with blood in the urine.</li><li><strong>Bowel and mesentery:</strong> crushed against the spine by seat belts or handlebars. Injury can show up late as <a href="/condition/peritonitis/">peritonitis</a>, with fever and worsening pain over 12 to 48 hours.</li><li><strong>Pancreas:</strong> injured by a direct upper-belly blow such as a handlebar. Symptoms can be delayed for days.</li><li><strong>Diaphragm:</strong> can tear, letting organs move into the chest.</li><li><strong>Bladder:</strong> especially if it is full at the time of injury or the pelvis is broken.</li></ul>
<h2 id="how-doctors-diagnose-abdominal-injuries">How doctors diagnose abdominal injuries</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Primary survey:</strong> airway, breathing, circulation and control of bleeding come first.</li><li><strong>Examination:</strong> of the whole belly, back, flanks, chest and pelvis, including looking for seat belt signs.</li><li><strong>FAST ultrasound:</strong> a quick bedside scan for free blood around the liver, spleen, pelvis and heart.</li><li><strong>CT scan with contrast:</strong> the main test in stable patients. It shows organ tears, active bleeding and free air from bowel injury.</li><li><strong>Blood and urine tests:</strong> blood count, liver and pancreatic enzymes, blood type, and urine for blood. Pregnancy test in women of childbearing age.</li><li><strong>Observation and repeat exams:</strong> a normal first CT does not always exclude bowel injury, so people are often re-examined over 12 to 24 hours.</li></ol>
<h2 id="treatment">Treatment</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Nonoperative management:</strong> most stable spleen, liver and kidney injuries now heal without surgery. You will be watched in the hospital with bed rest, repeat blood counts and scans.</li><li><strong>Angioembolization:</strong> an interventional radiologist blocks the bleeding vessel through a catheter passed from the groin.</li><li><strong>Surgery (laparotomy):</strong> for unstable patients with <a href="/condition/internal-bleeding/">internal bleeding</a>, bowel perforation, most gunshot wounds, and peritonitis. Damaged sections of bowel are repaired or removed, and the spleen may be removed.</li><li><strong>Blood transfusion</strong> and fluids for blood loss.</li><li><strong>Antibiotics</strong> when the bowel is injured.</li></ul>
<p>People who lose their spleen need vaccines against pneumococcus, meningococcus and Haemophilus influenzae type b, because they are more vulnerable to these infections for life.</p>
<h2 id="complications">Complications</h2>
<ul><li><a href="/condition/hemorrhagic-shock/">hemorrhagic shock</a> from uncontrolled bleeding</li><li>delayed rupture of the spleen days after the injury</li><li>peritonitis, abscess and sepsis from bowel injury</li><li>pancreatic fistula or pseudocyst</li><li><a href="/condition/bowel-perforation/">bowel perforation</a> discovered late</li><li>abdominal compartment syndrome, where swelling raises pressure inside the belly</li></ul>
<h2 id="recovery-and-returning-to-activity">Recovery and returning to activity</h2>
<p>After nonoperative care of a spleen or liver injury, most people avoid contact sports and heavy lifting for about 6 to 12 weeks, depending on the grade of injury. Return to normal activity after surgery takes 4 to 8 weeks or more. Seek help at once for new or worsening pain, dizziness or fever during recovery.</p>
<section class="faqs"><h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2><details class="faq"><summary><h3>What are the symptoms of a blunt abdominal injury?</h3></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Common symptoms are belly pain that worsens, tenderness or a rigid belly, bruising (especially from a seat belt), nausea or vomiting, blood in the urine, and left shoulder pain. Dizziness, a racing heart and pale, sweaty skin suggest internal bleeding and need 911.</p></div></details><details class="faq"><summary><h3>What does abdominal injury mean?</h3></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It means damage to the abdominal wall or to the organs inside the belly, such as the spleen, liver, bowel or kidneys. It is divided into blunt injuries, from blows and crashes, and penetrating injuries, from stab or gunshot wounds.</p></div></details><details class="faq"><summary><h3>Can internal bleeding in the abdomen be delayed?</h3></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. Bleeding can be slow at first and become obvious hours later, and a damaged spleen can occasionally rupture days after the injury. Bowel injuries may cause peritonitis 12 to 48 hours later. Get re-checked for any new or worsening pain.</p></div></details><details class="faq"><summary><h3>How do I know if a stomach injury after a fall is serious?</h3></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Warning signs are pain that keeps getting worse, a hard or swollen belly, vomiting, blood in urine or stool, shoulder tip pain, dizziness or fainting. A minor bruise that is improving, with normal eating and no other symptoms, is less worrying. When in doubt, get seen.</p></div></details><details class="faq"><summary><h3>What is a seat belt sign?</h3></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>A seat belt sign is a band of bruising across the lower belly or chest after a crash. It signals that a lot of force went through the belt, and it is linked to a higher risk of bowel, mesentery and lower-spine injuries. Anyone with it should have a CT scan.</p></div></details></section>
</div>
<section class="related" aria-labelledby="related-h"><h2 id="related-h">Keep reading</h2><div class="related-grid"><div><h3>Related symptoms</h3><ul class="chips"><li><a href="/symptom/abdominal-pain/">Abdominal Pain</a></li><li><a href="/symptom/sharp-abdominal-pain/">Sharp Abdominal Pain</a></li><li><a href="/symptom/blood-in-urine/">Blood in Urine (Hematuria)</a></li><li><a href="/symptom/shoulder-pain/">Shoulder Pain</a></li><li><a href="/symptom/bruising/">Bruising</a></li><li><a href="/symptom/back-pain/">Back Pain</a></li><li><a href="/symptom/nausea-and-vomiting/">Nausea and Vomiting</a></li></ul></div><div><h3>Related conditions</h3><ul class="chips"><li><a href="/condition/abdominal-trauma/">Abdominal Trauma</a></li><li><a href="/condition/spleen-injury/">Spleen Injury</a></li><li><a href="/condition/liver-injury/">Liver Injury</a></li><li><a href="/condition/internal-bleeding/">Internal Bleeding</a></li><li><a href="/condition/bowel-perforation/">Bowel Perforation</a></li><li><a href="/condition/kidney-injury/">Kidney Injury (Trauma)</a></li><li><a href="/condition/peritonitis/">Peritonitis</a></li><li><a href="/condition/hemorrhagic-shock/">Hemorrhagic Shock</a></li></ul></div></div></section>
<section class="sources"><h2 id="sources">Sources</h2><p>This guide was written from current patient and clinical guidance, including:</p><ul><li>American College of Surgeons: Advanced Trauma Life Support (ATLS)</li><li>MedlinePlus: Abdominal injuries</li><li>Eastern Association for the Surgery of Trauma: Practice management guidelines for blunt abdominal trauma</li><li>NHS: Major trauma</li><li>American College of Emergency Physicians: Abdominal trauma resources</li></ul></section>
